Values, types, and the type system.

Java is statically typed. That means every variable has a type fixed at compile time, and the compiler checks — before the program ever runs — that you only ever do type-appropriate things with it. Try to assign a string where a whole number is expected, and the code simply won't compile. That up-front checking is Java's core safety promise, and it starts with knowing the two families of types.

The first family is primitive types. These hold a raw value directly — a number or a bit, with no object wrapper around it. There are eight, but you'll use four constantly. There's int, a whole number. There's long, a bigger whole number, for values too large for an int. There's double, the everyday decimal number. And there's boolean, which is simply true or false. The other four — byte, short, float, and char, a single character — round out the set. The key idea is that a primitive variable is its value.

The second family is reference types — and that's everything else, all the objects. A reference variable doesn't hold the object itself. It holds a reference, a handle, to an object that lives out on the heap. Strings, arrays, and every class you'll ever write are reference types. So when you write int count equals forty-two, count is forty-two. But when you write string name equals "Ada", name points at a string sitting on the heap. That distinction drives a lot of Java's behaviour — how values are copied, how equality works, and why a reference can be null, pointing at nothing, while a primitive never can.

When you write values down, there are a few small rules. A long literal needs a capital L on the end. A char uses single quotes and holds exactly one character, while a string uses double quotes. And you're allowed to drop underscores into a long number, purely to make it readable.

Now, every primitive also has an object twin — int pairs with Integer, double with Double, boolean with Boolean. You need these wrappers whenever a slot demands an object rather than a raw primitive. The big example is collections: you write a list of Integer, never a list of int. Java converts between the two automatically, and that's called autoboxing. You add a plain forty-two to a list of Integer, and it's boxed into an Integer for you; you read it back, and it's unboxed to an int. It's convenient, but not free — a wrapper is a heap object, and unboxing a null will throw. So prefer primitives for plain arithmetic, and reach for wrappers only where an object is genuinely required.

Here's why all this static typing earns its keep. The compiler catches whole categories of mistakes before you ever run — misspelled fields, wrong argument types, impossible conversions. Your editor can autocomplete and refactor precisely because it knows the type of every expression. The cost is that you name your types. The payoff is that errors surface at compile time, instead of at three in the morning in production.
